LOGBOOK.md-220-- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets**. Rule **K2**: granted only where a **named second target** in the contract reads the same Data Protection Keychain item. Sharing is the entitlement's *only* function — it does not unlock the DPK, change durability, or survive anything the default group does not — so any argument for it that cannot name a second reader is an argument for something it does not do. On macOS there is no second reader: K1 excludes the root provider, and neither probe touches the DPK.
LOGBOOK.md-221-- FINDING: Withholding costs the host nothing, and Apple documents it rather than it being inferred. *Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps*: the `com.apple.application-identifier` entitlement is added to **every** app at signing; "the system recognizes this app ID as the name of your app's **default** keychain access group"; items in it are "**private to** App One"; "if you don't specify any keychain access groups, then **the app ID is the default**." TN3137 adds the macOS half: the access-group list is built from code-signing entitlements "**authorized by a provisioning profile**" — and `macos.host` is provisioned on both channels, so the injected identifier qualifies.
LOGBOOK.md-222-- FINDING (**the trap**): the entitlement array is ordered `keychain-access-groups` **first**, then the app identifier — the first element is the default when a caller names none. So withdrawing the entitlement does not only remove sharing, it **moves the default group**: `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared` → `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac`. An unqualified macOS write would have landed somewhere different before and after. Nothing is written yet so there is no migration, but `TASK-260715-379cpk` must not pass `kSecAttrAccessGroup` on macOS (it now returns `errSecMissingEntitlement`) while iOS still names the shared group explicitly — a platform branch, not a shared constant. **The verdict did not name this; it fell out of reading the source the verdict cited.**
LOGBOOK.md-223-- FINDING: Apple's documentation pages are JavaScript-rendered and fetch as title-only. The documentation **JSON endpoints** (`developer.apple.com/tutorials/data/documentation/<path>.json`) return the full text and are what made the quotes above obtainable.
LOGBOOK.md-224-- DECISION: `com.apple.security.application-groups` is now **prohibited on the iOS probe pair** (verdict 04 F3). r4 granted it because the probe "exercises the same shared-container path production depends on" — but `TASK-260715-1jckn0` scopes the probe to the `NETunnelProviderManager` lifecycle and versioned app messaging, its AC3 puts the whole exchange on `sendProviderMessage`, and no shared container appears anywhere. Rule G4 gained **`probeRule`**: a probe inherits **identifiers and profile classes, never entitlements**; if a probe is meant to prove a capability can be *provisioned*, that is a verification deliverable and must be an acceptance criterion **first** — the entitlement follows the criterion, not the reverse.
LOGBOOK.md-225-- DECISION: `group.works.relux.tunnel.probe` had exactly two consumers and both lost the grant, so **the record is deleted**, not deferred — a portal record no target reads is surface with no reader. Rule **G3** restated to the stronger property it now has: *no probe carries an App Group at all*, disposability by construction rather than by careful naming. One App Group record remains.
LOGBOOK.md-226-- FINDING: r1–r4 left all four **probe** keychain rows `prohibited` with **no reopening condition** — settled by *omission*, indistinguishable from an oversight and reopenable by anyone reading the silence as "nobody considered it". Found by the new R31 gate on its first run, not by inspection. Rows fixed, rule kept.
LOGBOOK.md-227-- FINDING: a negative gate caught a crash in its own new rule — re-granting the host *while* adding it to the record's consumer list made R31 raise `KeyError` (reading `e["value"]` on a granted row with no literal) instead of failing cleanly. Made crash-safe; a rule that throws reports nothing.
