LOGBOOK.md-262-### 0755 — An exhaustive entitlement allowlist inherits every entitlement the architecture implies, including ones the matrix only mentioned in prose (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r3)
LOGBOOK.md-263-- ROOT CAUSE: r2 argued in D-4 that `ReluxProxyMac` is sandboxed *because of* Sparkle, and quoted the spec clause naming the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach lookup exceptions — then never authored `com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name` as an entitlement row. The r2 containment assertion A10b declares every present key must be authored or signing-generated, so the allowlist would have **rejected a correctly built sandboxed Sparkle host**. Same defect class as r1's F3, one revision later: prose acknowledged the requirement, the machine-checkable set did not. The generalisable lesson is that the moment an assertion becomes *exhaustive*, every architectural consequence stated anywhere in the contract must also appear as data — rationale text is no longer a place a requirement can live.
LOGBOOK.md-264-- FINDING: Verified three ways before accepting. `.spec/platform-distribution.md:103-105` mandates the two exceptions; this repo's own `.research/260721_macos-self-update.md:111-113` records the identical two-value set independently; and `codesign -d --entitlements :-` across `/Applications` found six shipping apps carrying exactly the `<bundle-id>-spks` / `-spki` pattern, three of them (Mimestream, WhatsApp, Slipbox) sandboxed with those two values and nothing else.
LOGBOOK.md-265-- ANOMALY: **Tailscale carries the Sparkle exception while `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is `false`** — the same product cited as D-4 evidence that hosts are routinely unsandboxed. The exception is inert without the sandbox, so "shipping products carry this key" is not by itself evidence that a key is required. Worth remembering when mining installed apps for entitlement precedent: presence proves the shape is *accepted*, never that it is *needed*.
LOGBOOK.md-266-- FINDING: Sparkle 2's framework binary names **three** endpoint suffixes — `-spks`, `-spki`, `-spkp` — not two. The spec entitles two because the Downloader, Installer Connection, and Installer Status XPC services stay disabled. Recording this makes the two-value set legible as a deliberate least-privilege choice rather than an omission the next reviewer re-derives.
LOGBOOK.md-267-- DECISION: The row is attributed `project-architecture`, **not** `sandbox-consequence`, even though `com.apple.security.network.client` on the same target is a sandbox consequence and is equally Sparkle-driven. `network.client` follows mechanically from sandboxing alone; this key needs two independent project decisions to both hold (ADR-018's Sparkle choice **and** the host sandbox), so it is not derivable from "this target is sandboxed". Also added **rule X1**: the spec bans "unreviewed exception entitlement", so the contract now carries a reviewed-exception register plus an explicit banned set for Hardened Runtime relaxations — `get-task-allow` deliberately excluded from that set, since it is toolchain-injected on the development channel and governed by A10d instead. Without X1 this defect recurs one key at a time.
LOGBOOK.md-268-- VERIFICATION: 27 rules / 598 checks, exit 0, and **41 negative gates** (was 23) each re-introducing a defect and failing closed naming its rule — nine for the missing/mis-scoped exception, five for X1, four that delete or empty an assertion. That last group exists because the failure being fixed was an assertion set that did not cover its own contract; R27 now pins the assertion ids so the set cannot silently shrink again.
