LOGBOOK.md-270-### 0730 — Entitlement values are a property of the signing channel, not of the target (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
LOGBOOK.md:271:- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
LOGBOOK.md-272-- FINDING: Verified against three shipping Developer-ID packet-tunnel products installed on this Mac — Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark. All three carry the suffixed value on **both** the containing app and the embedded sysex, confirming the Developer ID half. No development-signed counterpart exists locally, so the development half rests on the DTS statement and is re-checked by assertion A3 at first signed build.
LOGBOOK.md-273-- DECISION: Every NE value in the contract is now keyed by channel (`development` / `developer-id` / `app-store`), each profile row records `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues`, and the C1 scope is pinned to development-channel values so the ceremony cannot be pointed at the wrong one. Consequence worth remembering: the probe family is development-signed only, so it can prove the **development** entitlement shape and nothing else — r1 implied it proved the production shape.
