o_reviewer-verdict-09.md.\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-83c1b4, pid=49397, exit=0)\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override (preferred_agentic_system: mixed[claude,codex], config: spawn.preferred_agentic_system)\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-17c375, max_parallel=1)\nspawn run started: [analyst] solution-architect (claude) (run=RUN-260728-17c375)\nr11 rework against reviewer verdict 09 (CHANGES REQUESTED, one blocking finding F1 plus required rework items 2-5). F1 was reproduced before it was accepted, against the UNMODIFIED r10 artifact and with the r10 validator and r10 rationale, because judging r10 data with the r11 gate proves nothing: all three coordinated-drift mutations exited 0 where a negative control must exit 1. CONFIRMED. No entitlement decision, identifier, target row, profile row, record, assertion, assertion scope, open constraint or Ceremony C1 mutation set changed, and X1-C1 itself is byte-for-byte what r10 reviewed - r11 changes how it is CHECKED. Rule X1 gains conditionalExceptionDerivation and R39 now DERIVES every field instead of comparing the register to its own mirror on the row: target from the unique row carrying the K3 version scope, key from K3's arming instruction, value set from a path-subtree bound that no coordinated edit can widen, channel set from the target's own channels in full, arming owner from K3's resolutionOwner cross-checked against the rule-D1 consumer list, review revision from the revision log, scope sentence as a rendered clause. The reviewed path and the bound vocabulary live in the gate, because a bound that lives only inside the data it bounds is not a bound. The item-3 sweep found two more of the same class the verdict had not named - a coordinated TARGET move to macos.host, and a reviewedIn naming a revision never issued - and one that had passed only by accident, key drift, which exited 1 under R35's assertion-scope partition rather than under the rule that owns the register. Item 4 was closed as a class, not as a string: the count was invisible twice over, because rule N1's only shape was '<n> keys' and both live sentences sat in scan-excluded regions, \u00a713 by exclusion and the preamble wholesale. N1 gains the harness-count shape, mutate.py counts its own entries and fails on disagreement, the validator requires the prose to render the declared value, and the preamble is now scanned with historical figures handled as named exclusions with reasons. That immediately surfaced a third stale number one paragraph away: \u00a79.2 claimed 36 internal consistency rules over R1-R36 while the file gates R2-R39 and has never had an R1; it is derived now too. Also found and reported rather than smoothed over: reviewer-gates.sh could not fail - its exit code was the trailing printf's, so it was 0 whatever the gates said, and four verdicts recorded that 0 as evidence; it now accumulates failures and was proved to exit 1 against a deliberately widened contract. r9's declared scan shape had already diverged from the compiled pattern. Two gates crashed instead of failing when their register was deleted; both now report under R38. Item 5 done: board resource metadata refreshed for the JSON, rationale, validator, mutation harness, board gate, both older preservation harnesses and the reviewer gates. Gates: validate-matrix.py 2805 checks exit 0; mutate.py 253/253 negative gates exit 0 with the declared R39 count matching the harness; check-portal-consumer.py 88 checks over 20 declared consumers exit 0; preserve.py 122, preserve-r10.py 201 and new preserve-r11.py 143 assertions exit 0; reviewer-gates.sh flips the r10 baseline false to r11 true and rejects