1872:### 1052 — Bumping a contract revision without a procedure that names its live consumers is how one of them ends up authorizing a rejected revision (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r7)
1873:- ROOT CAUSE: r5 stamped `TASK-260728-q5kjta`'s scope with "revision 2026-07-28.r5". r6 superseded r5 and **re-pointed one of two live consumers**, so the human authorization node named by AC5 went on authorizing the revision verdict 05 had **rejected**. Its mutation set was r6-correct — only the label was wrong. Reviewer verdict 06 F1.
1874:- ROOT CAUSE (the real one, one level up): the label was a symptom. `amendmentRule` required bumping `revision` and appending to `revisionLog` and **said nothing about which live board records must follow**. r6's own fix — a board gate — was aimed at the *mutation* consumer only, so the authorization edge was the one live consumer nothing checked. An amendment procedure silent on its consumers produces exactly this defect on every bump.
1875:- DECISION: `amendmentRule` now requires re-pointing **both** pinned consumers by task ID, and **R34 fails if it stops naming them or the word `re-point`**. The instance is fixed by a board edit; the class is fixed by the procedure being machine-checked. New rule **A1** (`authorizationNodeContract`) pins the authorization edge the way **P1** pins the mutation edge.
1876-- DECISION: nothing about the pin is written twice. The revision phrase is a **template** (`revision {revision}`) rendered from the contract's own `revision`, and R34 rejects a template or A1 field that hard-codes one; the banned superseded set is **derived from `revisionLog`**, so a bump extends the ban automatically; the excluded-identifier set is derived from `c1AuthorizationScope`.
1877:- FINDING: `notes` must be **excluded** from a staleness scan. Sweeping all **402** live board elements for a matrix revision label found two hits — the reviewer's finding, and this task's own `notes`, which the reviewer did not report and which is **not a defect**: append-only progress history where "r5 (2026-07-28.r5) closes all three verdict-04 findings" is a *true* statement. Same reason S1 excludes `revisionLog` from its count scan. The obvious "scan every field" implementation manufactures a failure out of correct history; A1 declares `excludedFields` and R34 fails if a field is ever both checked and excluded. The sweep is also what licenses the stronger claim: **no third live consumer carries a revision label**.
1878-- DECISION: the verdict's offered alternative — a content digest plus revision — **rejected with reasoning recorded in the contract** (`digestAlternativeRejected`). A digest changes on every JSON edit including ones that change nothing the operator approves, forcing a board write per revision without pinning anything the label does not, and a stale digest fails identically to a stale revision. **The gate closes the defect, not the pin format.**
1879:- FINDING (**a gate never observed failing is an assumption**): r6 introduced the board gate and only ever ran it in the passing direction, so nothing proved it would fail closed. 13 board negative gates now drive it from a *simulated* record — no live board record is mutated to test a gate — and each failure must be attributed to the **drifted consumer's own rule**, or a mutation to one consumer gets credited by a failure in the other.
1880-- REGRESSION (found by those gates, in the r6 gate itself): the right-boundary check `(?![\w.-])` rejected **every** following dot, so `Also create works.relux.tunnel.ios.` read as *no mention at all* — the r6 absence checks were defeatable by a **full stop**. Now `(?![\w-])(?!\.\w)`: a dot violates the boundary only when it *continues* the identifier. Strengthens rule P1 as well as A1.
1881-- REGRESSION (same source): a 220-character symmetric negation window let a neighbouring clause's negation launder an authorization — "`works.relux.proxy` is **migrated**" passed because AC4's earlier "explicitly **NOT** authorized" sat inside the window. A window wide enough to reach back over a list of four App IDs is necessarily wide enough to do this. Negation is now attributed **per clause**; clause splitting on `[.;:]\s+` and `,\s+(and|but|nor)\s+` is dot-safe because a dot *inside* these identifiers is never followed by whitespace. `negationWindow` was **removed**, not left as a field the gate reads and ignores — a dead field a gate still consults is the rot R32's dead-exclusion check exists to catch.
1882-- SCOPE: one board field on one element — `TASK-260728-q5kjta.scope`, revision label only, character delta **0**. `TASK-260715-3jloqy` needed no change and still passes P1. No element created, deleted, re-parented or re-linked. No portal mutation, no product source, spec file, or generated project touched.
