 | ADR-025 | Accepted | **Notarization credential custody is Keychain-only.** The only sanctioned runtime form of the App Store Connect notarization credential is a **named `notarytool` Keychain credential profile**; only that profile *name* may be recorded on the board, in the repo, or in CI configuration. The owner-supplied source `.p8` private key currently exists as a mode-0600 file on this host, which does not satisfy the Keychain-only invariant. Before the notarization path is declared ready, `TASK-260728-dveo1o` must (a) store the named profile, (b) verify it against the real notary service, and (c) record an explicit approved disposition for the source file — migration into Keychain-backed custody, retention under a named custodian with documented access control, or secure destruction. The file path, key ID, issuer ID, and key bytes are never echoed, logged, committed, or stored in board resources, and `set -x`/shell history must not capture the `store-credentials` invocation. CI keeps its own separately-scoped credential; the local profile is not exported. |
 | ADR-026 | Accepted | **Sparkle key generation is separated from updater integration.** The EdDSA keypair is generated and taken into custody at the up-front ceremony `TASK-260728-q5kjta`, because it depends on nothing; `TASK-260717-ziprhs` records the resulting public-key/custody evidence unattended. `SUPublicEDKey` pinning, CI signing-secret binding, and appcast sign/verify evidence are a distinct downstream task (`TASK-260728-3bj9bk`) blocked by the generated macOS target (`TASK-260715-uyju7n` → `TASK-260717-xempiv`) and the appcast pipeline (`TASK-260717-1mt4e7`). An accepted key ceremony never implies a working self-update signing path. |
 | ADR-027 | Accepted | **Deferral is expressed as `blocked` status, not as an unlinked backlog item.** A task deferred by owner decision — Gate A0 research (`TASK-260715-1o3q6l`), further `ReluxNIOSSH` fork work (`TASK-260715-1af33i`), iOS targets (`TASK-260715-33oofa`), physical-iPhone gates (`TASK-260715-1kntdx`) — carries `blocked` status with the evidence packet naming the deciding ADR and the exact input that would resume it. Leaving a deferred task in `backlog` with its blockers removed makes it *schedulable*, which is how prohibited work re-enters an autonomous run. Retained evidence and downstream links are preserved so the branch re-arms unchanged. |
-| ADR-028 | Accepted | **One human interaction is one board node, and a human node is never counted as autonomous.** (a) *Ownership.* A human sitting is owned by a single task; `TASK-260728-q5kjta` owns Ceremony C1 and is the only up-front human-input node. The evidence tasks it unblocks (`TASK-260715-apc34w`, `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, `TASK-260728-dveo1o`, `TASK-260717-ziprhs`) are agent work that runs unattended with the granted access and keeps its full evidence obligations. Spreading one sitting across several tasks forces the human to re-appear after each producer-reviewer cycle, which is what the round-2 review rejected. (b) *Labelling.* A task whose completion requires a human action or decision is a **human node** — a scheduler barrier that is never counted in an autonomous total, even when agent work follows inside the same node. Tasks that merely consume access or a decision granted earlier are autonomous and are reported as that node's follow-up. (c) *An approval that acknowledges a not-yet-existing artifact cannot be pre-granted*: Approval A1 (`TASK-260715-9yp8to`), sign-off S1 (`TASK-260715-2ayxqn` AC5), the manual ratification checkpoints (`TASK-260717-1dsqnj`, `TASK-260717-l639qp`, `TASK-260717-2d308k`), and the release-candidate approvals (`TASK-260715-1r48pc`, `TASK-260715-yynqbr`, `TASK-260715-2aessv`) are therefore separate, later interactions, not C1 content. |
+| ADR-028 | Accepted | **One human permission ceremony is one board node, and a human node is never counted as autonomous.** (a) *Ownership.* `TASK-260728-q5kjta` owns Ceremony C1 and is the only up-front human-input node. The evidence tasks it unblocks (`TASK-260715-apc34w`, `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, `TASK-260728-dveo1o`, `TASK-260717-ziprhs`) are agent work that runs unattended with the granted access and keeps its full evidence obligations. Spreading the permission ceremony across several tasks forces the human to re-appear after producer-reviewer routing, which is what the round-2 review rejected. (b) *Labelling.* A task whose completion requires a human action or decision is a **human node** — a scheduler barrier that is never counted in an autonomous total, even when agent work follows inside the same node. Tasks that merely consume access or a decision granted earlier are autonomous and are reported as that node's follow-up. (c) *Later-artifact rule.* An approval that acknowledges a not-yet-existing artifact cannot be pre-granted: Approval A1 (`TASK-260715-9yp8to`), sign-off S1 (`TASK-260715-2ayxqn` AC5), the manual ratification checkpoints (`TASK-260717-1dsqnj`, `TASK-260717-l639qp`, `TASK-260717-2d308k`), and the release-candidate approvals (`TASK-260715-1r48pc`, `TASK-260715-yynqbr`, `TASK-260715-2aessv`) are therefore separate, later interactions, not C1 content. (d) *Resumed evidence amendment, owner-approved 2026-08-10.* One ceremony node does not require one continuous sitting. C1 may combine resumed owner interactions with producer/reviewer work between them when the record states that provenance and does not invent uninterrupted-session boundaries, a historical Always Allow click, or separately timestamped two-factor completion. Acceptance rests on effective prompt-free access for the named signing identities/tools, an authenticated Relux Works portal/team session and confirmed matrix authority, named Keychain-backed notarization and Sparkle custody, an